#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_erfc

#% text
# `inverse_erfc(q)` gives the `x` with `erfc(x) = q`, for `0 < q < 2`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `inverse_erfc(1)` is 0; the value tends to `inf` as `q` tends to 0 and to `-inf` as `q` tends to 2.

#% item
# Small `q` keep their relative accuracy, so tail probabilities far below the machine epsilon can be inverted.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values; `inverse_erfc` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_erfc(0.5)
#% output
#= 0.4769362762044699

#% cell
inverse_erfc(1)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
inverse_erfc(1.5)
#% output
#= -0.4769362762044699

#% cell
n(inverse_erfc(1/10), 30)
#% output
#= 1.1630871536766740867262542605629476

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Very small tail probabilities:

#% cell
inverse_erfc(1.0e-100)
#% output
#= 15.065574702592645

#% cell
inverse_erfc([0.1, 0.01, 0.001])
#% output
#= [1.163087153676674, 1.8213863677184496, 2.3267537655135246]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `erfc` undoes it:

#% cell
erfc(inverse_erfc(1.0e-20))
#% output
#= 9.999999999999974e-21

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `erfc`, `inverse_erf`, `erf`
